<p>After a hurricane, hailstorm, or fire, a damaged building in Corpus Christi has to be repaired to the codes in force today, not the codes it was originally built under. On the coast, that also means meeting Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) windstorm requirements so the property stays eligible for Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) coverage. This guide explains Corpus Christi building codes for insurance repairs, what the city requires for re-roof permits, how WPI-1 and WPI-8 forms fit in, and how ordinance or law coverage can affect what your insurer pays.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Last verified: October 22, 2026</span><p>Codes, forms, and procedures change. This page is general information based on City of Corpus Christi and TDI sources, not code consulting or legal advice. Confirm current requirements with <a href="https://www.corpuschristitx.gov/department-directory/development-services/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Development Services</a> and <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/generalquestio.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDI</a> before starting work.</p></div>

<h2>Codes adopted by the City of Corpus Christi</h2>
<p>The City of Corpus Christi adopted a new set of construction codes, with local amendments, effective August 1, 2023, according to the city's <a href="https://www.corpuschristitx.gov/department-directory/development-services/construction-codes-and-ordinances/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">construction codes and ordinances page</a>. The codes are found in Chapter 14 of the City Code.</p>
