<tbody>
<tr><td>International Building Code (IBC)</td><td>2021</td><td>14-231</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Existing Building Code (IEBC)</td><td>2021</td><td>14-232</td></tr>
<tr><td>National Electrical Code (NFPA 70)</td><td>2020</td><td>14-241</td></tr>
<tr><td>Energy Conservation Code (IECC)</td><td>2015</td><td>14-251</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Fuel Gas Code</td><td>2021</td><td>14-261</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Mechanical Code</td><td>2021</td><td>14-271</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Plumbing Code</td><td>2021</td><td>14-281</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Residential Code (IRC)</td><td>2021</td><td>14-291</td></tr>
<tr><td>International Swimming Pool and Spa Code</td><td>2021</td><td>Adopted (see ordinance)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>One detail surprises people: the energy code is the <strong>2015</strong> edition of the International Energy Conservation Code, not the 2021 edition. The city's <a href="https://corpuschristitx.gov/media/2rpaite2/ordinance-2021-international-codes.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">adopting ordinance</a> states this in Section 1.2. Energy code provisions can matter on an insurance claim when replacing items such as insulation, windows, or HVAC equipment.</p>

<h2>Local wind design criteria</h2>
<p>The ordinance includes the local version of IRC Table R301.2, the climatic and geographic design criteria that guide residential construction. It lists:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Design wind speed:</strong> 140/150 mph</li>
<li><strong>Windborne debris zone:</strong> Yes</li>
<li><strong>Termite risk:</strong> Very High</li>
<li><strong>Elevation:</strong> 19 ft</li>
</ul>
<p>Being in a windborne debris zone typically affects requirements for protecting glazed openings such as windows and doors, and the wind speed drives fastening, connection, and component ratings. Termite risk can come up when framing or wood is exposed during repairs.</p>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Caveat on the posted ordinance</span><p>The ordinance PDF posted on the city's site has blank signature and date lines, so it may be the pre-adoption copy. The August 1, 2023 effective date comes from the city's construction codes web page. Ask Development Services to confirm the design criteria that apply to your address before relying on them.</p></div>

