<h2>Re-roof permits and the WPI-1</h2>
<p>Re-roofing requires a permit in Corpus Christi. According to the city's <a href="https://www.corpuschristitx.gov/department-directory/development-services/applications-permits-and-guidance/residential-remodels-and-repairs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">residential remodels and repairs page</a>, the roofing and siding submission includes:</p>
<ol>
<li>The Residential Building Permit Application (Form 1003)</li>
<li>A detailed scope of work naming the roofing method and materials</li>
<li>A completed TDI WPI-1 form from the windstorm engineer or inspector responsible for windstorm compliance</li>
</ol>
<p>The city's <a href="https://www.corpuschristitx.gov/media/5d1d0enr/homeowners-guide-to-permitting.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">homeowner permitting guide</a> describes re-roofing as replacing existing roof coverings and lists these items as needing a permit: replacing more than 100 square feet of shingles, changing the roof slope, removing and replacing roof decking, and changing the roof material.</p>
<p>The same remodel page notes that windstorm-related repairs, including interior remodeling and foundation repairs, require an engineer, and that new accessory construction requires a sealed windstorm plan. And the city's local amendment (Section 110.3.4.1) says framing inspections "do not include or take the place of inspections or certifications for compliance required under Texas Department of Insurance windstorm requirements."</p>
<p>For insurance claims, these requirements are real costs of the repair. Permit fees, engineering or windstorm inspection fees, and any work required to pass inspection may belong in the estimate, depending on the policy. Our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> page and our guide on <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof estimate</a> explain how to check for them.</p>

