<h2>The TDI windstorm program and WPI-8 certificates</h2>
<p>The TDI Windstorm Inspection Program was created by House Bill 2012 in 1987, and TDI inspections began January 1, 1988. It applies in the TWIA designated catastrophe area, which includes Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties, and the covered work includes "re-roofs or roof repairs." Inspections are performed by appointed Texas-licensed professional engineers or TDI inspectors.</p>
<p>Under <a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.2210.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Section 2210.251</a>, structures built, altered, remodeled, enlarged, or repaired, or with additions, on or after January 1, 1988 need a certificate of compliance to be eligible for TWIA coverage. TDI issues the WPI-8 and WPI-8-E certificates, and TWIA issues the WPI-8-C. TDI took over issuing certificates for completed improvements on June 1, 2020.</p>
<p>Starting <strong>April 1, 2026</strong>, TDI requires windstorm certificate of compliance applications (WPI-1) to be certified to the <strong>2024 International Residential Code or 2024 International Building Code</strong>, according to TDI's <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/adopted-codes.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">adopted windstorm codes page</a>. As TDI puts it, "Construction must be certified to be eligible for windstorm insurance coverage through TWIA." That means a Corpus Christi re-roof may need to satisfy the city's 2021 codes for the permit and TDI's 2024 codes for the windstorm certificate.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Who sets it</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Why it matters on a claim</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>City building permit (Form 1003, scope, WPI-1)</td><td>City of Corpus Christi</td><td>Re-roofs and other permitted work inside city limits</td><td>Permit fees and code-required work are part of the true repair cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>2021 IRC/IBC with local amendments</td><td>City of Corpus Christi</td><td>Construction in city limits since Aug. 1, 2023</td><td>Code upgrades may fall under ordinance or law coverage</td></tr>
<tr><td>WPI-1 application certified to 2024 IRC/IBC</td><td>TDI</td><td>Windstorm-regulated work from April 1, 2026</td><td>Required step toward the windstorm certificate</td></tr>
<tr><td>WPI-8 / WPI-8-E certificate of compliance</td><td>TDI</td><td>Work done on or after Jan. 1, 1988 in the catastrophe area</td><td>Needed for TWIA eligibility</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For more on certificates and claims, read our article on <a href="/windstorm-certificate-wpi-8-insurance-claims/">WPI-8 windstorm certificates and insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Ordinance or law coverage</h2>
<p>A common misconception is that if the code requires something, the insurer must pay for it. That is not how most policies work. Code requirements alone do not create coverage. Instead, many policies include <strong>ordinance or law coverage</strong>, sometimes as an endorsement, that pays up to its limit for the increased cost of complying with codes when repairing covered damage. The limit is often a percentage of the dwelling coverage or a fixed dollar amount, and some policies do not include it at all.</p>
<p>Examples where ordinance or law coverage may come up in Corpus Christi:</p>
<ul>
<li>Upgraded fastening, underlayment, or roof-to-wall connections needed to meet the 140/150 mph design wind speed</li>
<li>Impact-rated windows or opening protection in the windborne debris zone</li>
<li>Replacing undamaged portions of a system because the code does not allow a partial repair</li>
<li>Engineering and windstorm certification work required for TWIA eligibility</li>
</ul>
<p>Whether these are paid depends on the policy wording, the limit, and whether the underlying damage is covered. Some policies also require that the work actually be completed before the code-upgrade portion is paid. If your insurer's estimate leaves out code-required items, document the requirement (an inspection note, the permit conditions, or the specific code section) and ask the insurer to consider it under ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">claim supplements</a> pages explain that process, and our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> covers how depreciation and holdback payments interact with repairs.</p>

<h2>Other Coastal Bend communities</h2>
<p>The city codes above apply inside Corpus Christi city limits, including North Padre Island. Portland, Rockport, Port Aransas, Aransas Pass, Ingleside, Robstown, Kingsville, and unincorporated Nueces County set or administer their own rules, so check with that jurisdiction's building office. The TDI Windstorm Inspection Program applies throughout the catastrophe area regardless of city. See our <a href="/service-areas/">areas we serve</a> page for local notes.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster uses code information</h2>
<p>Corpus Christi Public Adjuster reviews your policy's ordinance or law limit, identifies code and windstorm requirements that apply to the repair, and includes them in an itemized estimate with supporting documentation. We are not code officials, engineers, or attorneys, and we do not make code determinations; we rely on the city, TDI, and your licensed contractor and engineer for that. For disputes involving lawsuits or deadlines to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If code items are missing from your claim, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call or text [[phone]]. As your Corpus Christi Public Adjuster, we can help you present those costs clearly.</p>
